  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through Thursday) \nIssued at 1126 PM CDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nThe shortwave trough will finally topple over the upper ridge and\nflatten as it slides east across the Central/Southern Plains over\nthe weekend. Latest model guidance suggests the best rain chances\n(20-40%) during the day Saturday will remain near and north of \nI-44 until the trough axis is able to crest the ridge axis, which \nlikely will occur overnight Saturday into Sunday morning. This is \nwhen the highest and most widespread precipitation chances \n(30-70%) will occur. With that said, still not expecting a wash- \nout of rain this weekend, as shower/convective activity should \nstay hit or miss with the exception of overnight Saturday. Though \nprecip chances get relatively high this weekend for many \nlocations, rainfall amounts will remain generally low and should \nremain below half an inch. \n\nConfidence continues to increase on a widespread rain event\nTuesday and Wednesday, and perhaps lingering into Thursday.\nConsensus in ensemble and deterministic global model data continue\nto show a robust upper-level trough and associated surface low\npressure/cold front will impact much of the Central CONUS mid to\nlate week. Latest model runs are a little more murky and\nindecisive with the evolution of the trough/upper low. Its\nevolution may be dependent on what is currently Hurricane Polo and\nwhat it does in the eastern Pacific as it moves inland sometime\nbetween Tuesday evening and Wednesday morning. The slower/delayed\ninland movement, depicted in the 12z run of the ECMWF (in\ncomparison to the GFS), slows the progression of the trough as it\nis ingested into the robust trough/upper low. If this verified,\nthis would keep the moderate to heavy rain chances lingering\nlonger as the trough/upper low slows down, as well as delay the\npassage of the cold front through the forecast area. Needless to\nsay, there is still a lot of uncertainty with regards to the\nsynoptic pattern beyond Tuesday. However, confidence remains high\nthat widespread moderate to heavy rain will occur mid to late\nweek, but detail will continue to be refined over the next several\ndays. \n\nMejia",
